================================================================================
Texas Instruments LPC10 speech data -- NOT redistributed
================================================================================

Plaits' five factory word banks are, in its own description, "LPC10-encoded
speech data extracted from Texas Instruments ROMs". The MicroFreak's banks
match them byte for byte.

This repository does not contain that data and must not come to contain it.
The tooling reads the banks out of a firmware image the user already owns,
at run time, and never writes them into source or into a committed artefact.
Keep it that way: the MIT licence covering the Plaits code around them is
not an obvious grant for speech recorded off a TI ROM, and nothing here
depends on redistributing it.


================================================================================
Renode -- MIT  (one peripheral model is derived work)
================================================================================

`renode/STM32F4DMA.cs` is a derivative work of Renode's own
`STM32DMA.cs` (Antmicro, MIT), by way of `STM32DMA_Fixed.cs` from
RallySmith/renode-issue-stm32f2-spi, an unmerged third-party fix attached to
renode/renode issue #615. What this project added is the half-transfer
interrupt (HTIF/HTIE) and half-buffer chunking for memory-to-peripheral
circular transfers; the file's own header says which is which.

Copyright (c) 2010-2023 Antmicro
Copyright (c) 2011-2015 Realtime Embedded

The MIT licence text those notices require is in `licenses/MIT.txt`.

The other five models in `renode/` -- `STM32_SAI.cs`, `STM32_QUADSPI.cs`,
`STM32_OTGFS.cs`, `STM32F4_Flash.cs` and `MicroFreakCodec.cs` -- are this
project's own work under the Apache-2.0 licence in LICENSE. They are written
against Renode's public peripheral interfaces, which is an API dependency and
not derivation. Where one exists because Renode's stock model could not do
what was needed, its header says so and names the stock model it replaces.

Renode itself is not redistributed here. It is a separate installation the
user supplies, found through `MF_RENODE`.
================================================================================
Arturia -- no code, no binaries, no affiliation
================================================================================

This project is not affiliated with, authorised by, endorsed by or connected
to Arturia in any way. "Arturia" and "MicroFreak" are trademarks of their
respective owners and are used here only to identify the hardware this
tooling was written for.

No Arturia code is included in this repository. The following are
deliberately absent, and are gitignored rather than merely missing: stock and
patched firmware images, extracted bootloader dumps, and the factory
vocabulary banks. Every tool that needs one reads it at run time from a copy
the user supplies themselves.

Stated plainly rather than glossed over: parts of this documentation were
written from recordings of the MIDI and SysEx traffic between the instrument
and Arturia's MIDI Control Center. Because a full MCC sync reads every slot,
those recordings contain factory preset parameter dumps as they appeared on
the wire -- knob positions and names, not code and not audio. **The recordings
themselves are not in this repository and nothing here needs them.** They were
how the project bootstrapped; a few documents still cite a path under
`captures/` when naming the evidence for a finding, and those paths are
outside this repository by design. What is published is the conclusion, not
the evidence.

One preset IS included: `tests/fixtures/init-patch.mfpz`. It is
the MicroFreak's blank state -- what the instrument writes when you press
Init and Save -- read off a device the author owns, and it exists so the test
suite can build the preset it needs instead of asking everyone to supply one.
It is the absence of a sound design rather than one, and no factory preset,
sample or wavetable is included.

It can be regenerated from any MicroFreak rather than taken on trust: press
Init, Save to a scratch slot, and read that slot back with microfreak-tools --
`mfreak preset read <slot> --out tests/fixtures/init-patch.mfpz`. The test that
uses it (`tests/test_encoder_roundtrip.py`) skips with that instruction when
the file is absent, so deleting it costs one skipped test and nothing else.

Documentation in this repository describes observed behaviour and gives
addresses within a firmware image. It does not reproduce the image.
